    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Eldred first appeared as 'Farrow Town' in 1830s river plat books, founded before Illinois statehood 1818.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eldred%2C_Illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Eldred was founded to combine river and bluff-side village values in Illinois River Bottoms.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eldred%2C_Illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Eldred sits in lower Illinois River Valley, 30 miles north of confluence with Mississippi, between limestone bluffs and fertile bottom land.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eldred%2C_Illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Illinois Route 108 runs through Eldred, leading east 8 miles to Carrollton and west 3 miles to the Illinois River and Kampsville ferry.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eldred%2C_Illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The 1920s drainage of the river bottoms created extensive farmland, a contentious but transformative change for the village.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eldred%2C_Illinois"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Eldred’s original founders were Scotch‑Irish from the Upland South, giving the village a distinct cultural heritage.",
